# MCSP Controlled Device Interaction 0.58

Status: Constructive isolated-runtime experiment
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Parents:
- CONTROLLED-KERNEL-EXECUTION-0.57.mcsp
- EMULATED-DMA-REALIZATION-0.35.mcsp

## Objective

Extend the qualified 0.57 Linux-kernel realization by one bounded, read-only
interaction with the emulated QEMU EDU device.

MCSP remains its own kernel:

    MCSP KERNEL = DETERMINATE CONDITION

Linux remains an external realization kernel.

## Report-derived observation contract

Target:
- PCI vendor 0x1234;
- PCI device 0x11e8;
- BAR0;
- register offset 0x00;
- access width 32 bits;
- operation READ only.

The installed QEMU 10.0.13 EDU specification defines BAR0 offset 0x00 as a
read-only identification register in the form 0xRRrr00edu, where RR and rr are
version fields.

0.58 therefore pre-settles only the stable identification suffix:

    observed_value & 0x0000ffff == 0x000000ed

The version fields remain realization-defined observations.

## Controlled path

    report-derived PCI identity
      -> MCSP assembly identity predicate
      -> Linux PCI probe
      -> pci_enable_device
      -> pci_iomap BAR0
      -> ioread32 BAR0+0x00
      -> MCSP assembly observation predicate
      -> CONDITION accepted or rejected
      -> pci_iounmap
      -> pci_disable_device
      -> guest termination

The assembly observation predicate determines whether the read value satisfies
the bounded report invariant. Linux performs the external-kernel mapping/read
mechanics but does not define MCSP semantics.

## Prohibitions

0.58 SHALL NOT:
- write any EDU MMIO register;
- start DMA;
- register or handle an IRQ;
- enable PCI bus mastering;
- mutate EDU device data;
- load an MCSP module into the host kernel;
- promote the observation into foundational STATE or TYPE.

Failure to map, read, or satisfy the predicate rejects the bounded probe.

## Qualification boundary

This checkpoint may qualify a single read-only MMIO observation of the emulated
EDU identification register through the MCSP condition path.

It does not qualify arbitrary MMIO, physical hardware, DMA, interrupts,
production deployment, or universal driver operation.
